  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current algorithm mostly relies on SLSQP with random shifts and noise for optimization, which may get stuck in local optima. The primary approach is to replace the local optimizer (SLSQP) with a global method (Dual-Accelerating Annealing, a variant of Simulated Annealing) designed to escape local minima. We leverage the provided parent solution and use diverse initialization strategies. The objective is to find a candidate with a lower C5 value (maximized correlation of h(x) and 1-h(x+k)) but note: in the problem, minimizing C5 directly improves the upper bound. We assume the parent solution is a local optimum.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation: h(x) is sampled at 280 points over [0,2], with dx=2/280 and sum(h_i)\u2248140.\n2. Objective: C5 = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,'full').max()) * (2/280) \n   This is the maximum cross-correlation value directly matched by the evaluator.\n3. Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = 140\n4. Duality: We use dual-annealing (a global optimization method) with:\n   - Bounds: Each h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Constraint: \u2211h = 140 (equality)\n5. Formulation: Equality constraint required since the integral enforcement must be exact. The method handles both bounds and equality.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start from the provided parent solution (if available).\n   - Generate up to 10 initial points using shifted versions of the parent with random shifts.\n2. Search:\n   - For each initial point:\n        \u2022 Use dual_annealing(optimizer) with:\n            * Method='dual_annealing'\n            * Options: \n                * initial_temp=0.1 (high to explore widely)\n                * final_temp=1e-7 (low for steady-state)\n                * maxiter=10000 (can be reduced if time runs out)\n                * maxfun=10000 (same)\n   - Track the best solution based purely on C5 value (lower is better).\n3. Validation:\n   - Each candidate must satisfy: \n        0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] \u2248 140 \u00b1 1e-6\n   - Evaluator compatibility: The C5 objective matches the builtin correlation calculation.\n4. Feasibility checks: Post-optimization clip values to [0,1] and enforce sum if needed (though the optimizer should theoretically handle it).\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- **Initialization diversity**: \n    \u2022 Parent solution: Zero trial (uses parent)\n    \u2022 Random shifts: 9 trials (shift by integer from -600 to 600)\n- **Optimizer parameters**:\n    \u2022 initial_temp: Varies from 0.1 to 0.3, tested if needed\n    \u2022 Maxiters: Dynamically adjusted if over 70% time used\n\nCompute budget:\n- Dominant cost: Dual-annealing iterations (10000 * 10 = 100K forced trials)\n- Feasible if at least 1-2 reasonable iterations occur\n- Check time after each trial, stop if total_time >= 0.99*budget_s\n\nValidation:\n- Always re-evaluate best candidate with builtin evaluator\n- Check value is finite and meet bounds/constraints\n- Return per the required interface\n\nValidation contract:\n- For returned h_values:\n    * np.min(h) >= 0\n    * np.max(h) <= 1\n    * abs(np.sum(h) - 140) <= 1e-4\n- Score (best_c5) must be \u2264 current best (0.38085)\n- n_points remains 280 (fixed parent resolution)",
